## Is endometriosis a disability?

Under the **Equality Act 2010**, a condition counts as a disability if it has a **substantial** (more than minor) and **long-term** (12 months or more) effect on your normal daily activities. The law looks at how you would be **without treatment**. Endometriosis that causes severe pain or fatigue can be covered. Many people with endometriosis are.

## Reasonable adjustments

- flexible hours, or working from home during flares
- easy access to a toilet
- time off for appointments and surgery
- a phased return after surgery
- not counting endometriosis-related absence towards sickness triggers

**Endometriosis UK** has an Endometriosis Friendly Employer scheme.

## Benefits

PIP is based on how endometriosis affects you on most days. Severe pain and fatigue can affect activities such as **preparing food**, **washing**, **dressing**, **toilet needs** and **moving around**. Think about how often you have bad days over a year.

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

## If you cannot work

If your health means you cannot work, or can only work a little, you may be able to get [New Style Employment and Support Allowance](/benefits/esa/) or extra money in [Universal Credit](/benefits/universal-credit-health/). These are separate from PIP, and you can get them at the same time.

Free, independent advisers, such as **Citizens Advice** or a local welfare rights service, can help you check what you can claim and fill in forms.
